<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · 1934 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Einheitsfrontlied</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A marching song from 1934 by Bertolt Brecht and Hanns Eisler, asking all workers to join together against the Nazis.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Bertolt Brecht</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Hanns Eisler</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the German labour movement; anti-fascist workers in exile</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>not known; first performed in Strasbourg, France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>December 1934; first performed 1935</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Bertolt Brecht, music by Hanns Eisler (1934))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einheitsfrontlied">Einheitsfrontlied</a>&quot; (United Front <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>) is one of the best-known songs of the German labour movement, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertolt-brecht">Bertolt Brecht</a> and set to music by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanns-eisler">Hanns Eisler</a>. It grew out of the long rift in the German left: since the split of the workers' parties in 1917 the Social Democratic Party (SPD), which upheld the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>, and the Communist Party (KPD), which rejected it, had been bitter opponents, and following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Comintern</a>'s line the communists denounced the social democrats as &quot;social fascists&quot;. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> came to power in January 1933 the Nazis banned both parties and the trade unions, and many, Brecht among them, came to believe that only a united front of social democrats and communists could still resist.</p><p>In December 1934, at the request of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> director Erwin Piscator, Brecht wrote the song for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">First International</a> Workers' Music Olympiad, held in Strasbourg in 1935, where a choir of 3,000 workers gave its first performance. The olympiad itself was marked by quarrels between communists and social democrats, despite the will to unity the song expressed. It was first printed and recorded in 1937, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, as &quot;Lied von der Einheitsfront&quot;, sung and published by the communist actor and singer Ernst Busch, and it appeared in Brecht's collection Svendborger Gedichte in 1939.</p><p>Eisler, who later composed the East German anthem &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auferstanden-aus-ruinen">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</a>&quot;, kept the setting deliberately simple so that workers without musical training could sing it; the result is close to a march. In 1935 he wrote to Marcel Rubin that it should be sung very simply, at a moderate pace and without false militant shouting. In 1948 he made a symphonic version, meant in part to distance the song from the march, a form fascism had tainted; Busch sang it for his Aurora project.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a> Ghetto in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> the song's text was the basis of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a> song: one account credits Shmerke Kaczerginski's &quot;Partizaner-Marsh&quot; of 1943, which refers to the Jewish resistance group FPO (Fareynikte Partizaner Organizatsye), another names a Yiddish version by Leyb Rozental. Later recordings include an instrumental &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the United Front&quot; on Charlie Haden's Liberation Music Orchestra (1969), Ton Steine Scherben (1971), a free-jazz version by Brötzmann, Van Hove and Bennink (1973), Hannes Wader (1977) and several punk bands.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to workers of every party to unite in a single front against fascism, grounded in their shared condition as workers; plain and march-like.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius" data-genius="219512"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press the button. <a href="https://genius.com/Bertolt-brecht-einheitsfrontlied-annotated" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
